<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The languages this app ships, declared so Android can actually resolve them.

  Without this file the app has no declared locale set, and Android negotiates a
  requested locale against nothing. Spanish and Arabic survive that, because
  their language subtag is unambiguous. **Chinese does not.** A bare `zh` has no
  script, Hans and Hant are different writing systems rather than dialects, and
  Android's negotiation will not guess between them. The request was dropped and
  the app fell back to English, silently, on a device set to Chinese.

  So a Chinese reader could not get this app in Chinese, and the four language
  catalogs that ship in `contract/i18n` were three as far as the platform was
  concerned. It was found by running the device in Chinese and reading the
  screen, which is the only way it could have been found: every test passes the
  locale explicitly, so every test proved the catalog and none proved the path
  a real person takes to it.

  Declaring it here also puts Health Trail in Android's per-app language picker
  under Settings, which is where a person actually changes an app's language.
  That was missing entirely.

  `zh-Hans` rather than `zh`, because that is the script the catalog is written
  in. `Strings.load` matches on the language subtag alone, so `zh-Hans` resolves
  to `zh.json` without any change there.

  Kamsiob, AGPL-3.0.
-->
<locale-config xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <locale android:name="en" />
    <locale android:name="es" />
    <locale android:name="zh-Hans" />
    <locale android:name="ar" />
</locale-config>
